essionally trapping and removing  pigs in the landscape you'll never catch  up that's because pigs are smart and if  you don't kill the entire group the ones  that survive can move locations and  teach their offspring how to avoid  hunters i think somebody said pigs are  the fourth smartest mammal but they're  probably smarter than about 80 percent  of the humans would be my best guess  so what'll happen is somebody's shooting  at them and they start seeing hunters in  tree stands if an animal has a bad  experience then they learn to avoid it  so their offspring can cue into those  behaviors and say hey you know mom says  this isn't a good situation  come on boys  in canada jurgen praichus saw the  problem looming decades ago come on be  easier for domestic pork producers like  him curbing the feral swine growth is  crucial to keeping his business and the  canadian economy afloat  the 73 year old is part owner of a pig  farm in mayor thorpe alberta jurgen says  he warned government officials in the  1980s of course i was pooh-poohed and  told that this was never going to be an  issue  wild boars were introduced into canada  in the 80s and 90s to diversify the  livestock industry over the years there  have been stories of boar producers  letting their stock loose while others  escaped and were able to multiply if you  have wild pigs like this come onto your  yard and then whatever disease they may  be carrying transmits to our animals  then it puts  the whole industry at risk feral pigs  can be a host to a list of diseases and  parasites like pseudorabies and the  hepatitis e virus one in particular is  called brucella it's a bacterial disease  and it can infect both humans and pigs  trichinolosis lives in the muscle of  pigs and can infect humans as well if  they ingest undercooked  pork brian stevens is a wildlife  pathologist at the university of guelph  in ontario  what is the threat to our domestic pig  population so asf is african swine fever  that is a viral d